{"records":[{"id":"3hxdrzfvsw","title":"Transcription-coupled AID Deamination Damage Depends on ELOF1-associated RNA Polymerase II","description":"In adaptive immunity, transcription-coupled damage is introduced into antibody genes by activation-induced cytidine deaminase (AID) to diversify antibody repertoire. However, the coordination between transcription and DNA damage/repair remains elusive. Here, we find that transcription elongation factor 1 (ELOF1) stabilizes paused RNA polymerase II (RNAPII) at transcription barriers, providing a platform for transcription-coupled DNA damage/repair. Using a genetic screen, we discover that ELOF1 is required for AID targeting and that ELOF1 deficiency results in defective antibody class switch recombination and somatic hypermutation in mice. While downstream transcription-coupled repair factors are dispensable for AID-damage, ELOF1 mechanistically facilitates both transcription-coupled damage and repair by stabilizing chromatin-bound RNAPII. In ELOF1-deficient cells, paused RNAPII tends to detach from chromatin and fails to recruit factors to induce or repair DNA damage. Our study places ELOF1 at the center of transcription-coupled DNA metabolism processes and suggests a transition of RNAPII from elongation to a DNA damage/repair scaffold.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-02-05T15:02:00.618Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/3hxdrzfvsw","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/3hxdrzfvsw"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"鹏飞","last_name":"代","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Yifeng","last_name":"Luo","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Western Blot"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"wp9sf6sp4z","title":"Study of STK19 drives Transcription-Coupled Repair by stimulating repair complex stability, Pol II ubiquitylation and TFIIH recruitment by Ramadhin et al. ","description":"Raw data of figures in our manuscript 'Study of STK19 drives Transcription-Coupled Repair by stimulating repair complex stability, Pol II ubiquitylation and TFIIH recruitment'  by Ramadhin et al. ","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2024-10-01T08:23:08.193Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/wp9sf6sp4z","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/wp9sf6sp4z"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Jurgen","last_name":"Marteijn","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Cell Biology"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"vth6c4x9pp","title":"A single RNA polymerase II ubiquitylation site coordinates transcription with the DNA damage response","description":"In response to transcription-blocking DNA damage, cells orchestrate a multi-pronged\nreaction, involving transcription-coupled DNA repair, degradation of RNAPII and\ngenome-wide transcription shutdown. How these responses are connected has\nremained unclear. Here we show that damage-induced ubiquitylation of RNAPII itself,\nat a single lysine (RPB1 K1268), is the focal point for DNA damage response\ncoordination. K1268-ubiquitylation affects DNA repair and signals RNAPII degradation,\nessential for surviving genotoxic insult. It is also crucial for transcriptional shutdown,\nin the absence of which cells display dramatic transcriptome alterations. Additionally,\nregulation of RNAPII stability is central to transcription recovery – indeed, depletion of\nthe RNAPII pool underlies the failure of this process in Cockayne syndrome B cells.\nThese data expose regulation of global RNAPII levels as integral to the cellular DNA\ndamage response, and open the intriguing possibility that RNAPII pool size generally\naffects cell-specific transcription programmes, in genome instability disorders and\neven normal cells.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2019-12-20T16:44:11.686Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/vth6c4x9pp","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/vth6c4x9pp"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Ana","last_name":"Tufegdzic Vidakovic","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Vesela","last_name":"Encheva","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Bram","last_name":"Snijders","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Jesper","last_name":"Svejstrup","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Mass Spectrometry","Expression Proteomics"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"b5nxzsvd39","title":"Transcription-coupled DNA repair underlies variation in persister awakening and the emergence of resistance ","description":"Persisters constitute a population of temporarily antibiotic-tolerant variants in an isogenic bacterial population and are considered an important cause of relapsing infections. It is currently unclear how cellular damage inflicted by antibiotic action is reversed upon persister state exit and how this relates to antibiotic resistance development. We demonstrate that persisters, upon fluoroquinolone treatment, accumulate oxidative damage which is repaired through nucleotide excision repair. Detection of the damage occurs via transcription-coupled repair using UvrD-mediated backtracking or Mfd-mediated displacement of the RNA polymerase. This competition results in heterogeneity in persister awakening lags. Most persisters repair the oxidative DNA damage, displaying a mutation rate equal to the untreated population. However, the promutagenic factor Mfd increases the mutation rate in a persister subpopulation. Our data provide in-depth insight in the molecular mechanisms underlying persister survival and pinpoints Mfd as an important molecular factor linking persistence to resistance development.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2022-03-02T10:59:11.212Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/b5nxzsvd39","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/b5nxzsvd39"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Dorien","last_name":"Wilmaerts","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Microbiology","Antibiotics","Escherichia coli","Antibiotic Resistance"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"77k5xyhtjs","title":"Efficient RNA polymerase II pause release requires U2 snRNP function. Caizzi*, Monteiro-Martins* et al.","description":"Transcription by RNA polymerase II (Pol II) is coupled to pre-mRNA splicing, but the underlying mechanisms remain poorly understood. Co-transcriptional splicing requires assembly of a functional spliceosome on nascent pre-mRNA, but whether and how this influences Pol II transcription remains unclear. Here we show that inhibition of pre-mRNA branch site recognition by the spliceosome component U2 snRNP leads to a widespread and strong decrease in new RNA synthesis from human genes. Multiomics analysis reveals that inhibition of U2 snRNP function increases the duration of Pol II pausing in the promoter-proximal region, impairs recruitment of the pause release factor P-TEFb, and reduces Pol II elongation velocity at the beginning of genes. Our results indicate that efficient release of paused Pol II into active transcription elongation requires formation of functional spliceosomes, and that eukaryotic mRNA biogenesis relies on positive feedback from the splicing machinery to the transcription machinery.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2021-02-25T23:59:27.865Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/77k5xyhtjs","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/77k5xyhtjs"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Livia","last_name":"Caizzi","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Sara","last_name":"Monteiro-Martins","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Genomics","Systems Biology","Transcription","RNA Splicing"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"xwfh7rnzc2","title":"DNA - directed termination of RNA Polymerase II transcription","description":"RNA polymerase II (RNAPII) transcription involves initiation from a promoter, transcript elongation through the gene, and termination in the downstream terminator region. In bacteria, terminators often contain specific DNA elements provoking polymerase dissociation, but RNAPII transcription termination is thought to be driven entirely by protein co-factors. We used biochemical reconstitution, single-molecule studies, and genome-wide analysis in yeast to study RNAPII termination. Unexpectedly, transcription into terminators by pure RNAPII results in spontaneous termination at specific sequences containing T-tracts. Single-molecule analysis indicates that termination involves pausing without backtracking prior to dissociation. The ‘torpedo’ Rat1-Rai1 exonuclease (XRN2 in humans) greatly stimulates spontaneous termination but is ineffectual on other paused RNAPIIs. By contrast, elongation factor Spt4-Spt5 (DSIF) suppresses termination. Genome-wide analysis further indicates that termination occurs by transcript cleavage at the polyA site exposing a new 5’ RNA-end that allows Rat1-Rai1 loading, which then catches up with destabilized RNAPII at specific termination sites to end transcription. ","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2023-09-08T10:19:23.492Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/xwfh7rnzc2","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/xwfh7rnzc2"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Zhong","last_name":"Han","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Gene Transcription","Termination of Transcription"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"j3p84sb38k","title":"MDC1 maintains active elongation complexes of RNA polymerase II","description":"Current biochemical data indicate a functional interplay of MDC1 DNA damage response scaffold protein with the transcription and splicing machinery both in unchallenged conditions and upon DNA damage. The PDF file includes wider areas of scanned western blots which are already included in the main manuscript entitled ''MDC1 maintains active elongation complexes of RNA polymerase II''. ","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2022-12-01T17:28:50.485Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/j3p84sb38k","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/j3p84sb38k"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Georgios ","last_name":"Pappas","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Western Blot"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"fc56g3m8ff","title":"KAP1 is a Chromatin Reader that Couples Steps of RNA Polymerase II Transcription to Sustain Oncogenic Programs","description":"Collection of western blots and SDS-PAGE images","source":{"id":"f881433a-b1cd-40d2-ab9f-4fd501f2d14b","name":"Elsevier Data Repository","external":false},"publication_date":"2020-02-20T19:38:08.978Z","url":"https://elsevier.digitalcommonsdata.com/datasets/fc56g3m8ff","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/fc56g3m8ff"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Ivan","last_name":"D'Orso","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Biochemistry","Molecular Biology"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"ybmr387wyv","title":"Aberrant accumulation of Kras-dependent enhancer RNAs during tumor progression renders cancer cells susceptible to PAF1 depletion","description":"KRAS is the most commonly mutated oncogene in human cancer and mutant KRAS is responsible for over 90% of pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma (PDAC), the most lethal cancer. Here, we show that RNA polymerase II associated factor 1 complex (PAF1C) is specifically required for the survival of PDAC but not normal adult pancreatic cells. We show that PAF1C maintains cancer cell genomic stability by restraining the over accumulation of enhancer RNAs (eRNAs) and PROMPTs driven by mutant Kras. Loss of PAF1C leads to cancer-specific lengthening and accumulation of pervasive transcripts on chromatin, and concomitant aberrant R-loop formation and DNA damage, which in turn trigger cell death. We go on to demonstrate that the global transcriptional hyperactivation driven by Kras signaling during tumorigenesis underlies the specific demand for PAF1C by cancer cells. Our work provides insights in how enhancer transcription hyperactivation causes general transcription factor addiction during tumorigenesis.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2023-08-09T11:01:29.655Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/ybmr387wyv","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/ybmr387wyv"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Mo","last_name":"Chen","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Xinhong","last_name":"Liu","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Xiangzheng","last_name":"Liu","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Yingxue","last_name":"Du","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Di","last_name":"Zou","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Chen","last_name":"Tian","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Yong","last_name":"Li","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Xun","last_name":"Lan","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Charles","last_name":"David","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Qianwen","last_name":"Sun","orcid":null}],"funders":[{"id":"https://ror.org/027s68j25","name":"Ministry of Science and Technology of the People's Republic of China","grant_id":"2020YFA0509402"},{"id":null,"name":"Natural Science Foundation of China","grant_id":"92068114"},{"id":"https://ror.org/027s68j25","name":"Ministry of Science and Technology of the People's Republic of China","grant_id":"2021YFA1300100"}],"categories":["Cell Biology","Tumor Biology","Pancreatic Cancer"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"p9mpgv3y4v","title":"LINF_200005000","description":"DNA-directed RNA polymerase II subunit 2 | RPC2; Leishmania infantum (strain JPCM5)","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-07-15T18:41:32.388Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/p9mpgv3y4v","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/p9mpgv3y4v"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Jose M.","last_name":"Requena","orcid":null}],"funders":[{"id":"https://ror.org/003x0zc53","name":"Agencia Estatal de Investigación","grant_id":"PID2020-117916RB-I00/AEI/10.13039/501100011033"}],"categories":["Gene","Protein","Leishmania"],"journal_issn":[]}],"total_records":95205,"page":1,"page_size":10,"execution_time":94,"aggregations":{"data_type_stats":{"dataset":95205,"collection":0},"access_type_stats":{"open":94946,"restricted":49,"embargoed":210},"top_funders_stats":[{"id":"https://ror.org/01h0zpd94","name":"National Natural Science Foundation of China","count":1494},{"id":"https://ror.org/003x0zc53","name":"Agencia Estatal de Investigación","count":505},{"id":"https://ror.org/01cwqze88","name":"National Institutes of Health","count":485},{"id":"https://ror.org/03ha2q922","name":"National Science Centre","count":353},{"id":"https://ror.org/03swz6y49","name":"National Council for Scientific and Technological Development","count":316},{"id":"https://ror.org/02ddkpn78","name":"Fundação de Amparo à Pesquisa do Estado de São Paulo","count":311},{"id":"https://ror.org/00x0ma614","name":"Coordenação de Aperfeicoamento de Pessoal de Nível Superior","count":309},{"id":"https://ror.org/021nxhr62","name":"U.S. National Science Foundation","count":276},{"id":"https://ror.org/018mejw64","name":"Deutsche Forschungsgemeinschaft","count":232},{"id":"https://ror.org/00k4n6c32","name":"European Commission","count":177}],"source_stats":[{"id":"MENDELEY_DATA","name":"Mendeley Data","count":92858},{"id":"f881433a-b1cd-40d2-ab9f-4fd501f2d14b","name":"Elsevier Data Repository","count":1790},{"id":"a191461c-0227-403f-a1e9-621e0e0429cc","name":"San Raffaele Open Research Data Repository","count":152},{"id":"b07018a5-fee6-4df1-9d43-ff0d81ad75ed","name":"Bicocca Open Archive Research Data","count":90},{"id":"2a9d1b7d-e447-466f-888f-aa6f84e575e4","name":"Digital Commons Data@Becker","count":61},{"id":"df228bbb-ac66-410f-8c66-f61608d63d95","name":"Univ